    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_07d5d9836f9abcc186e367302d394e5f360d8e52d8d6c36a3a2894bfd4218013",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The abstract curve proof and its arithmetic specialization share a multiscale engine: localize separated pieces, Taylor-reduce their phases to quadratic normal forms, use nondegeneracy to obtain product coordinates, apply planar L6 decoupling, and propagate the local gain by recurrence. In the discrete specialization, periodicity and translated anisotropic boxes provide additional normalization and aggregation, while recursive separated-versus-concentrated interval decomposition converts the resulting bilinear control to the linear mean-value estimate.",
