    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3587b38757ae422f1aaed0d023238950ea24f247150571d964204e38b720fcaa",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The proof's intermediate scales are interfaces between analytic modules. In the curve decoupling argument, K determines a smaller scale at which quadratic approximation and planar decoupling feed a recurrence; in the bilinear-to-linear argument, a sufficiently large fixed K makes the recursive coefficient contract after separated pairs are removed. In the exponential-sum application, V trades a narrower second-spacing window for a larger adjacent sieve factor, while N is optimized—and then reduced to the feasibility boundary N comparable to R^2—to balance the spacing bounds against admissibility.",
