    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8e7bb637549f40f6024d884b6ff30ae42778816c8027cc39807b072e9b304238",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The bounded-range estimate becomes an exponent-pair statement only after three additional coverage operations. Complementary estimates fill the lower-alpha gap, scale rescaling and the Poisson/B-process reduction handle sums with M above T^(1/2), and a smooth phase extension followed by interval restriction removes the assumption that the phase and sum occupy the full dyadic interval. Together these operations make the original exponent bound portable across the parameter and domain presentations required by exponent-pair theory.",
